==English==

{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|grc}} stem of {{term|πρᾶγμα||act|slang=grc|tr=pragma}} + {{term|-ism}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈpɹaɡmətɪzəm/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|-}}

# The pursuit of [[practicality]] over [[aesthetic]] qualities; a concentration on [[facts]] rather than [[emotion]]s or [[ideal]]s.
# {{politics}} The [[theory]] that political [[problem]]s should be met with practical [[solution]]s rather than [[ideological]] ones.
# {{philosophy}} The [[idea]] that [[belief]]s are identified with the [[action]]s of a believer, and the [[truth]] of beliefs with success of those actions in securing a believer's [[goal]]s; the [[doctrine]] that ideas must be looked at in terms of their practical [[effect]]s and [[consequence]]s.
#* '''1902''', William James, ''The Varieties of Religious Experience'', Folio Society 2008, p. 378:
#*: Our conception of these practical consequences is for us the whole of our conception of the object [...] This is the principle of Peirce, the principle of '''pragmatism'''.

====Antonyms====
* [[idealism]]
* [[contemplation]]

